    "created_at": "2016-11-26T14:08:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-11-26T14:08:36Z",
    "body": "> Why not also validate the demos?\r\n\r\nThe demos lack `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">` so are not guaranteed to validate under all circumstances.\r\n\r\nWasn’t sure we want to add `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">` to them but I’ve opened #2103 now to do that.\r\n\r\nBut I also now did 04f4674 to check the demos regardless of whether we decide to merge #2103.\r\n\r\nHowever without #2103 if we ever switch to the checker HTTP POST API for remote checking rather than using the `vnu.jar` command-line tool on them locally, the demos won’t validate. Details below.\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nIf it’s best practice to always include `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">` I guess the demos should follow that.\r\n\r\nThat said, the demos as-is will validate if we have Travis check by running `vnu.jar` on them locally.\r\n\r\nThe reason is, `vnu.jar` basically assumes all files it’s given from the command line locally are UTF-8 and only emits an error if when it’s parsing them it finds they’re not actually UTF-8.\r\n\r\nThat behavior’s for pragmatic reasons—because in that command-line case there’s no `Content-Type` header, so if a file lacks `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">` then `vnu.jar` would otherwise emit this error:\r\n\r\n    error: The character encoding was not declared. Proceeding using “windows-1252”.\r\n\r\nSo I made `vnu.jar` not emit that error for files lacking `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">`, and instead behave as if the document is (will be) served with a `Content-Type:text/html; charset=utf-8\r\n` header.\r\n\r\nBut the demos as-is will **not** validate if we instead use the HTML checker’s HTTP POST API:\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ curl -s -F out=gnu -F doc=@demos/canvas/blue-robot/index-idle.html https://checker.html5.org\r\n\"index-idle.html\": error: The character encoding was not declared. Proceeding using “windows-1252”.\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe difference is because unlike the `vnu.jar` command-line behavior, the checker’s HTTP POST API doesn’t (currently) assume all POSTed documents it gets are UTF-8.\r\n\r\nSo the checker HTTP POST API will emit an error for any document lacking `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">`.",
